  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how JavaScript brings websites to life by making them respond to user actions such as clicks, typing, or hovering. Through hands-on comparisons of good and bad design, they connect interactivity to user-centered design. Students take on the perspective of UX designers to understand how interactive features shape user experience and often require making trade-offs.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nExplore how interactivity impacts the overall user experience of a webpage and learn how to consider design trade-offs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make a web app respond when I click?**\n\nStudents connect everyday cause-and-effect to event-driven code: they select an element → attach a click listener → update the DOM (e.g., change text). They study a working model, debug broken handlers with a human-first checklist, verify any AI suggestions, and then author their own handler, establishing the core pattern used in later lessons on variables and conditionals.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of Making a Button Respond activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make a web app respond when I click?**\n\nYou’ll wire up a button so a click changes what you see on the page using a simple recipe: select → listen → update. You’ll try a working example, fix one that’s broken, and then write your own, asking AI for hints but keeping only code you understand."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how programs track and update changing values using variables, operations, and state. Through an unplugged simulation, guided code exploration, and AI-assisted debugging, they connect abstract programming concepts to real-world interactions. The lesson emphasizes reasoning about program behavior, verifying AI suggestions, and maintaining consistency between stored data and what appears on screen.",
        "preparation": "- Gather blank sheets of paper",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nIn this lesson, you explore how apps keep track of changing information using variables and updates. You act out how values change, look at how code makes those changes happen, and use AI to help debug and understand how data in a program connects to what you see on the screen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how writing reusable functions helps make code cleaner, easier to maintain, and more readable. They rename and refactor functions to improve clarity, then create and test arrow functions with AI support to practice abstraction and collaboration. Through peer feedback and reflection, students connect clear naming and organization to the QA Engineer lens, recognizing how readability supports both human and AI understanding of code.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nIn this lesson, you make your code cleaner and easier to reuse by writing and renaming functions. You create arrow functions, refactor repeated code, and give clear names so your programs — and your collaborators — can easily understand what your code does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nStudents connect conditional logic to real-world app behavior by modeling decisions with flowcharts and translating them into JavaScript if/else statements. Through peer feedback and AI-assisted debugging, they strengthen their understanding of how code can make choices that affect user experiences.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Print copies of the unplugged Crafting Conditionals with Flowcharts activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nModel and code real app decisions by turning your flowchart ideas into JavaScript programs that respond differently based on user actions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Functions with Parameters",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make functions more flexible by passing information into them?**\n\nIn this lesson, students extend their work with functions by introducing parameters for flexibility and reuse. They will use their pattern recognition skills to identify reusable processes and possible parameters, moving from flowchart to code. Students author and test their own parameterized function, using AI for hints and debugging while remaining accountable for understanding and verifying any suggested code.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Print copies of the Functions with Parameters activity guide if your students will not use Sketch Lab",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make functions more flexible by passing information into them?**\n\nYou will learn how to make a function more flexible and reusable using parameters—- one or more variables that are passed into a function. This knowledge will allow you to convert many specialized functions into more general functions that can make your code more efficient and organized. You will use AI for hints and debugging while remaining accountable for understanding and verifying any suggested code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Building More Dynamic Pages",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app make more dramatic changes to the page?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how accessibility and interactivity intersect by designing webpages that respond dynamically to user needs. Acting as UX Designers, students identify common accessibility challenges, connect them to user benefits, and use JavaScript to make responsive updates—changing styles, toggling elements, and applying variables and event-driven logic. Through collaborative testing and reflection, they consider the trade-offs developers and designers face when balancing accessibility, usability, and design aesthetics.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Building More Dynamic Pages activity guide (one per student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app make more dramatic changes to the page?**\n\nIn this lesson, you take on the role of a UX Designer to make a webpage more accessible and interactive. You identify a user’s needs, plan a feature to help them, and use JavaScript to make the page respond dynamically. The lesson highlights how small coding changes can make a big difference for all users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditionals for Complex Logic",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app handle multiple layers of decisions?**\n\nStudents extend their understanding of conditional logic by creating programs that use nested if-else statements to handle conditions that depend on other conditions. They plan two-diamond flowcharts, translate them into JavaScript, and test all possible outcomes to ensure their logic behaves as intended.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Nested Conditionals activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app handle multiple layers of decisions?**\n\nYou’ll learn how to make programs that use nested if-else statements to handle situations where one condition depends on another. You’ll plan a two-step flowchart, turn it into JavaScript, and test each possible outcome to be sure your logic works correctly."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app check more than one condition at the same time?**\n\nStudents build on their knowledge of conditional logic by learning AND and OR operators. They learn how to fill out a truth table for all possible outcomes and diagram out a flowchart with multiple outcomes. They turn their flowcharts into code and test out all possible True/False combinations to make more robust web pages.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Combining Conditions with AND/OR activity guide (one for each student)\n- (Optional) Print copies of the unplugged Creating a Multi-Decision Flowchart activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app check more than one condition at the same time?**\n\nLearn how to chain together multiple conditionals using AND and OR operators to account for a larger range of user actions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I design and debug more complex conditional rules?**\n\nIn this lesson, students will learn how to incorporate the NOT operator to make more complex conditionals. They will learn the Flip State Pattern in order to create boolean toggles that render UI changes within web applications. As they draft more complex conditionals, they will also reflect on the inclusivity and the fairness of their chosen conditions for various subgroups.",
        "preparation": "- Print the Opposite Simon Says Statements resource",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design and debug more complex conditional rules?**\n\nYou will use the NOT operator to write more complex conditionals. You will learn the Flip State Pattern to build toggles that change what you see on the page. You will create a small feature that uses a toggle, test it, and fix issues you find. You will also check that your conditions are fair and inclusive for different users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nIn this 5-day project, students design and build an interactive web app that reacts to user input using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Through milestones that move from planning and algorithmic logic to coding, testing, and reflection, students practice computational thinking and iterative design.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Reactive Logic Project Planning Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nIn this project, you’ll create your own interactive web app that responds to user actions in real time. You’ll plan your app’s logic, build and test its code using JavaScript, and refine it through feedback and reflection. By the end, you’ll have a finished app that shows how your ideas, logic, and creativity work together to make something dynamic and fun."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
