  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "671a54a9-13d0-4ed8-ad90-dc5d6bde0e0f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "671a54a9-13d0-4ed8-ad90-dc5d6bde0e0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1582a47e-8cf1-4c4b-abb6-d81a9af8673e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a34a21e5-cec5-4f85-b79c-a5180d21608c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we're going to explore how adding JavaScript brings websites to life and makes them respond to user actions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a34a21e5-cec5-4f85-b79c-a5180d21608c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "007aab13-8707-4956-9c84-4ceec35b61b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b54d79a-006a-437f-9a2c-87f39fd6c30e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the image on this page and ask:\n- What parts of this site do you think are interactive?\n- How do users interact with it?\n\nIf students are unsure about which features are interactive, ask prompting questions such as: “What buttons, menus, search bars, or icons do you see?” “What do you expect will happen when you click/tap/hover on it?”",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If possible, open a sample website and do a live demo of interacting with different elements, such as the search bar, clickable buttons, and dropdown menus. Here is the website used in the image on the slide: [**https://usa.tommy.com/en/men**](https://usa.tommy.com/en/men)"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b54d79a-006a-437f-9a2c-87f39fd6c30e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "007aab13-8707-4956-9c84-4ceec35b61b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6b69af7-fd1a-46df-b0e2-f12ddf5f75d8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6b69af7-fd1a-46df-b0e2-f12ddf5f75d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "007aab13-8707-4956-9c84-4ceec35b61b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "035625e5-9dca-4382-94c4-b1588b4b4d8c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the image on this page and ask:\n- What parts of this site do you think are interactive?\n- How do users interact with it?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What clues did you find that indicated an element was interactive?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Have students reflect on how common elements, words, or icons on a page indicate interactivity. For example, a magnifying glass indicates a search bar, a carrot symbol ^ indicates a dropdown, and a ribbon indicates being able to save something.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "035625e5-9dca-4382-94c4-b1588b4b4d8c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "007aab13-8707-4956-9c84-4ceec35b61b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e3a6d15-7549-4c76-adda-2863d356b342",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will experience and articulate the difference between good and bad interactive design, connecting it to user experience.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Interactivity in Web Apps"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e3a6d15-7549-4c76-adda-2863d356b342",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b82e7292-593f-4676-818d-f5bf8f090240",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you're a User Experience (UX) researcher. We're going to try out two different versions of an app to see how design impacts how we feel when we use it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b82e7292-593f-4676-818d-f5bf8f090240",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21c81813-aa63-4082-aa01-f4d4b02616aa",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 and 2. Explain that they will be exploring two versions of a web app. On Level 1, they will note any features or functionality that are unclear, inconsistent, or frustrating. On Level 2, they will note how those features were improved.\n",
        "progression_name": "Explore a Web App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21c81813-aa63-4082-aa01-f4d4b02616aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2dabc8eb-1a61-408c-a800-388f22703a61",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Interactivity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider guiding students through the first interactive demo, which should be intentionally frustrating. Show them how the “Save” button moves around when you try to click it, that you have to click the “Add” button twice in rapid succession, and how the “Shuffle” button just changes the background color. Ask them if they notice any other features of the site that could be improved. After they have seen the demo, transition them to try it out on their own.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2dabc8eb-1a61-408c-a800-388f22703a61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bdb853b7-695c-45fc-9112-230c126da124",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bdb853b7-695c-45fc-9112-230c126da124",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c492a567-1865-4f1e-bc5c-ac01b2bf242c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you feel using the first version of the web app? Why? What design choices made the second version better?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect their feelings of frustration or ease to specific design elements like button placement, clarity of icons, navigation flow, and responsiveness. Guide them to use specific examples from their experience. Listen for students identifying issues like 'the button kept moving' or 'I didn't know where to go next' for the bad design, and 'it was obvious what to do' or 'each button did what I expected it to' for the improved design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define interactivity.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c492a567-1865-4f1e-bc5c-ac01b2bf242c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf0ab2ea-be58-474d-9d14-e68f6b21a8a2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "UX designers think about how buttons, menus, and animations can guide users smoothly, make actions feel clear and responsive, and create an experience that’s enjoyable and easy for everyone to use.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf0ab2ea-be58-474d-9d14-e68f6b21a8a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffc9ce4a-b7e4-492f-b560-9394a28749e5",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will consider features that benefit one group of users but may frustrate others, for example, autoplay vs. manual start.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 3 and choose one panel level to look at. Encourage them to think like UX designers making a decision about whether or not to incorporate a new feature. What tradeoffs will they have to make?\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Trade-Offs in Design",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Trade-Off"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffc9ce4a-b7e4-492f-b560-9394a28749e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0299cfae-6417-42f4-a31e-c7a06ded6b42",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Hear From Different Users"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0299cfae-6417-42f4-a31e-c7a06ded6b42",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13a7ed93-11e5-40ba-8691-6519a932f2f0",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define trade-offs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you designed a new website with the feature you explored, what trade offs would you have to make?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to identify specific design features and articulate the conflicting needs they serve. Guide the discussion towards understanding that there is rarely a single 'perfect' design, and that design decisions often involve balancing competing priorities. Listen for students identifying trade-offs like 'Autoplay is good for users who want to consume content quickly without thinking, but it's bad for users who have limited data or prefer to choose what they watch.'\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure to have students who looked at different features share out, i.e. infinite scroll, confirmation messages, and autoplay. Alternatively, pair students up to discuss in small groups with a classmate who looked at a different feature.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13a7ed93-11e5-40ba-8691-6519a932f2f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "593920ac-b458-47bb-9cbe-9d4d3e82caca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6d5e812-84fe-4936-aa9d-fc78bad0a2c2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6d5e812-84fe-4936-aa9d-fc78bad0a2c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a89dec26-22fe-475b-90e6-0f2e0f4c8844"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a40ee3a-ee23-429e-aff5-29ee73f05993",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a40ee3a-ee23-429e-aff5-29ee73f05993",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a89dec26-22fe-475b-90e6-0f2e0f4c8844"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bee6c55-5717-41fc-b17d-dc496f1544ce",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one small action you took today that caused something else to happen?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify a cause and its resulting effect. Listen for examples like: pressing a button to turn on a light, opening a door to enter a room, or tapping a screen to unlock a phone. Guide students to see that each 'event' (the trigger) caused a 'response' (the system's reaction).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bee6c55-5717-41fc-b17d-dc496f1544ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b779c63-aad6-409b-a779-00401afee1cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a77bca82-ba17-4272-96ae-dadc3819b352",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Students should each have a copy of the Making a Button Respond activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define DOM. Explain that the DOM turns static elements on a page into objects that users can interact with.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1 where students will explore an interactive page with buttons. They will describe how they interact with the page and attempt to document the corresponding code in script.js. Students should use AI to help understand the code by asking questions like, 'What does [this line of code] mean?' They can attach specific lines of code as context within the AI chat interface.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Making a Button Respond",
        "progression_name": "Button Response",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Introduce the concept of code comments as post-it notes for humans. Comments serve as explanations or directions for engineers reading through the code.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a77bca82-ba17-4272-96ae-dadc3819b352",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "521b7da8-32af-4dd5-9cbd-fb6b6412723f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "521b7da8-32af-4dd5-9cbd-fb6b6412723f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cac31ce1-16fc-4a05-b0a1-63c9bdb6b816",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define event handler. Explain that an event handler defines the reaction that a program takes based on a specific action, like a button click.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What event handlers did you trigger in Level 1?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to understand that clicking a button requires an event handler to trigger a reaction. Students should note where in their code they see document.querySelector and addEventListener which indicate onscreen content manipulation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 2, where they will act as QA Engineers, debugging broken code both by following a prescribed checklist, and then with AI assistance. Remind them to first try their own strategies and to ask AI specific questions like 'Why isn't this button working?' rather than asking for the full solution.\n",
        "progression_name": "Fixing Broken Features",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This level highlights that AI can make mistakes and reinforces the importance of understanding code logic. Encourage students to use their checklist provided to check the work before relying on AI. Guide the discussion towards common issues like typos in IDs, missing parentheses, or incorrect property names.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cac31ce1-16fc-4a05-b0a1-63c9bdb6b816",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "863b9d29-ed85-4af5-9c42-1ec4e0e5e525",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "863b9d29-ed85-4af5-9c42-1ec4e0e5e525",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ca428d2-a08b-4c49-9e84-ed1bb3068607",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Share one error you found and how you were able to debug it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** To reinforce learning through peer explanation and to highlight common pitfalls. Encourage students to explain the error and the solution in their own words, modeling effective communication and collaborative problem-solving.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students pair up and verbally explain the intended functionality of their event handler before they attempt to run the code. This 'pair check' helps them catch logical errors.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ca428d2-a08b-4c49-9e84-ed1bb3068607",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37cbbbc0-6487-48b0-b8b7-758b2a0776eb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37cbbbc0-6487-48b0-b8b7-758b2a0776eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71906712-5145-4fe6-836b-8b6e2a244c20",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 3 to complete Levels 3 and 4.\n- Level 3: Students write working code for getting a button click to respond, using AI for assistance.\n- Level 4: Students will create a simple app that utilizes multiple buttons.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Coding Clickable Buttons",
        "progression_name": "First Click!"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71906712-5145-4fe6-836b-8b6e2a244c20",
