    {
      "key": "1d58ab3c-023f-4dd3-b1e3-ed5d5a5a8250",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Event Handler",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that AI is a tool to assist, not to do the work for them. They should try to solve problems independently first before consulting AI. Emphasize asking specific questions to get the most helpful AI responses.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d58ab3c-023f-4dd3-b1e3-ed5d5a5a8250",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "222a8059-52e7-4a92-a382-be3db18dec43",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "222a8059-52e7-4a92-a382-be3db18dec43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2be15d5e-c028-4a93-8c8c-19559e8132c9",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students of the three-step process that they followed in order to update the DOM when a user clicks a a button in a web application: Select an element, add an event listener, and update specific content (text, image, color, etc.)\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2be15d5e-c028-4a93-8c8c-19559e8132c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "214536c0-cdc2-46c9-aaae-2606e43b74e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6ebe10c-d23d-4fdf-b951-c80ff1dd0209",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review Key Vocabulary\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6ebe10c-d23d-4fdf-b951-c80ff1dd0209",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a50cddf6-5030-49b9-a0b5-87a6d66b87c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cca456a7-f630-446f-804d-504522b10604",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cca456a7-f630-446f-804d-504522b10604",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a50cddf6-5030-49b9-a0b5-87a6d66b87c5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ca371bb-d0e1-48d0-893b-2b9ff38148e6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Likes and Dislikes buttons on the screen. Give students 10–15 seconds to think, then ask a few to share quick observations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Submit Form example. Give students 10–15 seconds to think about what happens behind the scenes, then invite a few quick responses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the Comments Section visual. Give students a few seconds to consider how new comments appear, then invite 2–3 responses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Where else have you seen elements on a page that track and update a value?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify examples of dynamic content that changes based on user interaction or data. Examples include: view counts, likes, scores, timers, progress bars, etc. Guide the discussion to connect these observable changes to underlying data that is being tracked and updated.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ca371bb-d0e1-48d0-893b-2b9ff38148e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6f6159b-45a3-4719-be15-ee1723af0dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51412cd2-9eb0-43f3-9023-6b4c99fb029d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of 2-3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute blank pieces of paper for each group to use for the changing values simulation.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience how a value can change repeatedly while still being tracked — modeling the concept of a variable.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Changing Values"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51412cd2-9eb0-43f3-9023-6b4c99fb029d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d829ac93-e718-461e-bcea-b29ef3204c0e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s act out how something keeps track of changes over time. You’ll see how the number changes again and again — but we’ll always know the most recent one.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d829ac93-e718-461e-bcea-b29ef3204c0e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d6e7f35-bc0b-4728-a127-e520bf2115a1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the simulation activity to students and use the animated slide to show an example of what each role will do. As you click through, point out how one student will write the starting number, and others will take turns giving new numbers to add or subtract. The animation should help students visualize what they’ll write on their papers and how the number changes each time.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you have a group of two, have one student take the role of both addition and subtraction.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d6e7f35-bc0b-4728-a127-e520bf2115a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4b1423d-df77-455b-8950-529ace9cceb8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4b1423d-df77-455b-8950-529ace9cceb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "730a2a38-6356-4d88-9aa0-f1e3463cef42",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the simulation. One student writes score = 0 on their paper. The “+” and “–” students take turns giving new numbers to add or subtract, while the score student crosses out the old number and writes the new one each time. Encourage students to say the new score aloud so everyone  follow the changes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe students as they act out the changing values. Check that the score student is correctly updating the number and not keeping the old values. Ask prompting questions like: “How do you know what the current score is right now?” or “What happens to the old number once a new one is written?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, pause midway and ask: What pattern are you noticing as the number keeps changing? This primes them to connect what they’re doing to the concept of something that stores and updates information later.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "730a2a38-6356-4d88-9aa0-f1e3463cef42",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22d52274-86bc-411e-827d-567756f5a7d2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22d52274-86bc-411e-827d-567756f5a7d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5a104a8-794f-46ba-9912-87148f94aeba",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When we changed the number on the paper, what did that paper represent?*\n- *How is that like what a computer keeps track of in a program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify that the paper’s number acts like a stored value that can change — a bridge to the idea of a variable. The paper keeps track of the most recent value, just as a program stores and updates data over time.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5a104a8-794f-46ba-9912-87148f94aeba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e641020-83f8-40ce-9cd0-6578bdd5eab3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s put some names to what we just acted out. In code, that paper’s number is like a variable, and the current number it shows is called its state. Let’s see what that means.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e641020-83f8-40ce-9cd0-6578bdd5eab3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a486303-69ba-4b44-8d76-282b8c0e4d09",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define variable. Connect the definition back to the simulation they just did by explaining that the paper held a number that kept changing — like a score that went up and down. In programming, we call that paper a variable because it’s a container that holds one value at a time. That value can change as the program runs.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck on the idea of a variable as a container, help them make the connection by explaining how you can take out what’s inside and replace it with something new — but you only see one thing at a time.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a486303-69ba-4b44-8d76-282b8c0e4d09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de2db977-f183-4293-b08f-39f309bcfa17",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de2db977-f183-4293-b08f-39f309bcfa17",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8468fd66-abe1-4c8e-a8cf-59adb2f1cca1",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define state for students and connect it back to the simulation and the variable definition by explaining how our variable’s number kept changing — but at any given moment, it only showed one value. That current value is called the state. It describes what’s happening right now in the program.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You can compare this to a snapshot of a game or app at one moment in time. For example: “Right now my score is 200, I have 2 lives left, and the level is 3. That’s the program’s current state.”  This helps students see how state reflects a real-time picture of what the program knows at that exact moment.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8468fd66-abe1-4c8e-a8cf-59adb2f1cca1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6dfafbf9-c65b-42a4-8727-aa837da0fb57",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6dfafbf9-c65b-42a4-8727-aa837da0fb57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a61fff02-2b2b-4f0f-9ef9-47e4b269217a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define operation for students. Connect this concept to the earlier simulation by explaining that when someone added or subtracted a number, that was an operation. In code, operations are what make variables change. For example, adding one to your score when you click a ‘like’ button.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students notice that “score” appears twice, explain that’s how the program knows which value to update. The variable’s name on both sides of the equals sign connects the old value to the new one.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a61fff02-2b2b-4f0f-9ef9-47e4b269217a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a0c9cda-a30a-4e9e-9c16-dfdb25cdb6f5",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a0c9cda-a30a-4e9e-9c16-dfdb25cdb6f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b0f22d5-0779-4197-ac50-0a7fc16a1a89",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore a working app and predict how it keeps track of a changing score.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1 and have them explore the app by clicking the + and – buttons. As they experiment, prompt them with questions like: “What do you think is happening behind the scenes?” and “Where do you think the score is being stored?” Encourage partners to describe what they see changing and what they think the code might be doing to make that happen.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Working with Variables",
        "progression_name": "Counter App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b0f22d5-0779-4197-ac50-0a7fc16a1a89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62d573f0-4db9-400b-9527-41c37ead10c7",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** After students discuss their predictions, reveal the code for the Score Tracker app (or direct them to open script.js). Point out the variable declaration, the operations that add or subtract from the score, and how that number is displayed on screen.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students guess incorrectly, use their ideas to highlight real debugging logic: “That’s a great guess! Let’s check the code and see if it works that way.”  This keeps the tone exploratory and reinforces that programmers test their predictions by reading code.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62d573f0-4db9-400b-9527-41c37ead10c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "911139b5-8853-4571-a67f-1f4de8aa3e5a",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "911139b5-8853-4571-a67f-1f4de8aa3e5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a781034-89b2-4c8d-af42-f3113c985712",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use AI as a coding partner to comment and debug code, reinforcing understanding of variables, state, and operations through reflection and iteration.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 9. Remind them that in these levels, they’ll see how the app’s code matches what they’ve been acting out.\n- Level 2: Students work with AI to learn about the different parts of the code and make comments.\n- Level 3: Students read the vocabulary definition for variable.\n- Level 4: Students add their own explanation or example of variable to the vocab free response.\n- Level 5: Students read the vocabulary definition for state.\n- Level 6: Students add their own explanation or example of state to the vocab free response.\n- Level 7: Students read the vocabulary definition for operation.\n- Level 8: Students add their own explanation or example of operation to the vocab free response.\n- Level 9: Students use AI to help identify and fix bugs in the program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to listen for students using AI productively. Ask:\n- “What did the AI tell you this part of the code does?”\n- “Does that explanation make sense to you?”\n\nUse these interactions to check for understanding of how variables are updated and displayed.\n",
        "progression_name": "Commenting Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a781034-89b2-4c8d-af42-f3113c985712",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0679e937-e8d6-4157-af5b-f4026e0935e6",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Variable"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0679e937-e8d6-4157-af5b-f4026e0935e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d79e6ae-68c4-4baa-9ca6-1891a8eddf6b",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: State"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d79e6ae-68c4-4baa-9ca6-1891a8eddf6b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99504de3-c948-4201-ae50-89be86490eb1",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Operation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99504de3-c948-4201-ae50-89be86490eb1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e179abed-08c3-4daf-9f8b-4ee4b12ec9bb",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Debugging Variables",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that AI is a learning tool — it can suggest ideas or explanations, but students are responsible for confirming the logic. You might say: AI is great at spotting patterns, but you’re the one who has to decide if the pattern really fits.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e179abed-08c3-4daf-9f8b-4ee4b12ec9bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd188e8c-abb9-406f-8ee8-562835afc364",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd188e8c-abb9-406f-8ee8-562835afc364",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c7abbd0-ac2a-4a54-934e-a9ad41f9afad",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When you were debugging, how did you figure out whether the value on the screen matched the program’s state?*\n- *How did AI help you find or fix bugs — and when did you have to step in to make your own decision?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to connect debugging with verifying that the program’s state (variable values) and what appears on screen are consistent. Encourage students to mention the four key debugging checks they may have practiced:\n- Name check: Did I spell the variable name right and use it consistently?\n- Value check: Does the variable have the value I expect? (Try console.log() to confirm.)\n- DOM check: Am I changing the correct element or class on the page?\n- Order check: Does the code that updates the page run after the variable’s value changes?\n\nThese checks help students reason about how and when the program updates information. Also connect this back to AI debugging: AI can help spot likely errors because it recognizes patterns, but students still need to verify the logic — especially when the pattern doesn’t quite fit.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students bring up cases where the AI’s suggestion didn’t fully solve the problem, highlight that moment as valuable: it shows they’re taking ownership of testing and verifying — a professional debugging mindset.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c7abbd0-ac2a-4a54-934e-a9ad41f9afad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd343e0c-65fa-4074-80fb-c13d82532d33",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd343e0c-65fa-4074-80fb-c13d82532d33",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28c82436-5ea7-4313-aa70-4f3059bf6dcb",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 10 and have them select one of the available projects to complete. Explain that each project uses variables differently — for example, tracking music beats, social interactions, or mood states.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask them to explain which variable they chose, how it changes, and what causes the change. Encourage them to connect their app’s feature back to a real-world example (e.g., a like counter or score tracker). As you see students using AI Chat to troubleshoot, prompt them to:\n- Describe their AI prompt out loud.\n- Evaluate whether the AI’s suggestion actually fits their goal.\n\nRemind students that AI is a tool to help them learn, not just fix problems — they should still verify and understand the reasoning behind any code changes.\n",
        "progression_name": "Build Your Own Web App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, have them use AI Chat to brainstorm two new features for their project that would involve variables. Have them choose one AI-suggested idea to implement and test. Encourage them to evaluate whether it’s practical and enjoyable for users.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level provides a strong formative assessment of student understanding. Use it to check whether students can:\n- Declare and update variables correctly.\n- Explain how their variable changes during the app’s execution (reflecting understanding of state).\n- Use AI purposefully — asking targeted questions, testing solutions, and verifying correctness.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28c82436-5ea7-4313-aa70-4f3059bf6dcb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9001a4e2-8626-4a32-b520-bc1a5ef929af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5f07693-412f-4974-91ba-fedb9e59a940",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What is the difference between updating a variable and updating the DOM?*\n- *How do these two actions work together in your app?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that updating a variable changes the program’s internal data (its state), while updating the DOM changes what the user sees on the screen. The goal is for them to realize that these two processes are connected but distinct — the DOM reflects the variable’s value, but it doesn’t update automatically. Students should leave the discussion recognizing that keeping variables and the DOM in sync is an essential part of making interactive web apps work correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When was a time that you trusted your own debugging process over AI?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that while AI can be a powerful debugging partner, their own reasoning, testing, and understanding of the code are essential for verifying solutions. The goal is for them to realize that AI sometimes assumes patterns that may not apply, and human judgment is needed to confirm accuracy. This discussion helps students develop confidence in their debugging process and understand AI as a tool to collaborate with—not rely on blindly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 11 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5f07693-412f-4974-91ba-fedb9e59a940",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cfbcebfe-7d3b-47fc-ae4b-fab3084d387d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bf51826-1d67-4282-93c3-a526315bf3c2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bf51826-1d67-4282-93c3-a526315bf3c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cfbcebfe-7d3b-47fc-ae4b-fab3084d387d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17440603-0d0a-4015-87b6-485d81fb2202",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Working individually or with a partner, they should jot the steps down on a scrap piece of paper in the order they think makes the most sense. After ordering, students will give their set of steps a short, descriptive name and share how they decided on the order.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the slide and remind students that the steps are intentionally out of order. Instruct students to jot them down on a scrap piece of paper in the order that makes the most sense to them. Let them know that there isn’t one “right” sequence. After arranging their steps, they should give the process a short, descriptive name and share with a neighbor how they decided on the order. Then, ask a few students to share with the class. Emphasize how giving their set of steps a clear name makes it easier for others to understand what it does—this will connect directly to how programmers name and reuse functions\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students have different ideas about the first step, use that as part of the discussion. Highlight that algorithms can vary depending on context and user goals."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17440603-0d0a-4015-87b6-485d81fb2202",
        "lesson_activity.key": "026861d2-cae0-4b96-a5db-4e2a2ff32570"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "990a364b-6c7e-411d-a78e-a489eb6f5fb6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "990a364b-6c7e-411d-a78e-a489eb6f5fb6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "026861d2-cae0-4b96-a5db-4e2a2ff32570"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c514aee-fc63-45bd-9834-16a8e164f47b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect their activity to the new vocabulary. Explain that what they just did — naming and reusing a set of steps — is exactly what programmers call a function. When we reuse those steps to make complex processes easier, we’re practicing abstraction.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c514aee-fc63-45bd-9834-16a8e164f47b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "026861d2-cae0-4b96-a5db-4e2a2ff32570"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e30c60f-220d-4647-ae20-84543821f596",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In programming, we call a reusable set of steps a function. It lets us do something again and again without rewriting all the details. When we reuse steps to simplify a bigger task, that’s called abstraction — it helps us focus on what matters instead of every tiny detail.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students know they’ll see the official definitions of function and abstraction in Levels 2–5, where they’ll have a chance to apply and revisit them. This slide is just meant to connect the warm-up to the coding concepts they’re about to explore.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e30c60f-220d-4647-ae20-84543821f596",
        "lesson_activity.key": "026861d2-cae0-4b96-a5db-4e2a2ff32570"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e405999-baa0-485e-afbb-2372a506a7f3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e405999-baa0-485e-afbb-2372a506a7f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "026861d2-cae0-4b96-a5db-4e2a2ff32570"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7e461b5-8aa8-4fb4-a836-6a62f5823c56",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7e461b5-8aa8-4fb4-a836-6a62f5823c56",
        "lesson_activity.key": "026861d2-cae0-4b96-a5db-4e2a2ff32570"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5ffadfe-2889-4961-aba5-a487d807ebf0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students rename functions with descriptive, meaningful names and reinforce their understanding by adding or refining comments in their code.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Exploring Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5ffadfe-2889-4961-aba5-a487d807ebf0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31a3ccd7-043b-415b-997f-8048184fa4ee",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Front-end developers create code that others can easily read and reuse. One way to make code clearer is through refactoring—cleaning up and renaming parts of the code to reduce duplication. Today, you’ll act as front-end developers by making your code more readable and useful to others.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31a3ccd7-043b-415b-997f-8048184fa4ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "133a1d2f-654f-4422-9625-7abf4f317502",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 5.\n- Level 1: Students rename functions descriptively and add helpful comments.\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for function.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of function to the vocab free response.\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for abstraction.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of abstraction to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, listen for the reasoning behind their renaming choices. Ask guiding questions like: “Why did you choose that name?” or “Would someone new to this code understand what that function does?”\n",
        "progression_name": "What's in a Name?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "133a1d2f-654f-4422-9625-7abf4f317502",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a8ea785-ed5a-41ee-be3d-62c0963123e0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Function"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a8ea785-ed5a-41ee-be3d-62c0963123e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd1d9dae-08bc-4444-82fc-8abed84be91b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Abstraction",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to think about readability for both humans and AI. Ask: “If AI read your code, would it be able to tell what each function does based on your naming?” This connects the task to professional readability and accountability goals."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd1d9dae-08bc-4444-82fc-8abed84be91b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f5b7e71-ceb9-477e-9826-d69d53f0c783",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f5b7e71-ceb9-477e-9826-d69d53f0c783",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb0858a5-80e9-42ca-9eb2-f295d9006cde",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why do descriptive names for functions matter?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that descriptive names make code easier to read, understand, and maintain—for both themselves and others. Help them connect this to abstraction, explaining that a good name lets programmers focus on what a function does instead of how it works.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share one example of a renamed function and explain why they chose that name. Highlight the importance of clarity and collaboration—well-named functions help teams (and even AI tools) interpret code more accurately.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb0858a5-80e9-42ca-9eb2-f295d9006cde",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37ba15c4-290d-402a-8858-f847eb8b3559",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37ba15c4-290d-402a-8858-f847eb8b3559",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54c30ddf-ef7f-41e3-8f2b-82dc9f5673a3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show examples of functions and point out the similarities:\n- Both perform the same action (returning \"Hello!\").\n- The difference is mainly in syntax — arrow functions use => instead of the word function.\n- We still give them clear names and use them to reuse code.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54c30ddf-ef7f-41e3-8f2b-82dc9f5673a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cbaeee40-6354-4170-8ea7-91e1a62d6bf3",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the next few levels, you’ll start seeing functions that use arrows instead of the word function. Don’t worry — they work exactly the same way. The arrow just makes the code shorter and easier to read.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you sense hesitation, have students point to the parts that match between the two styles (name, parameters, body). Reinforce that programmers use both versions, but arrow functions are often preferred for concise, modern code.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cbaeee40-6354-4170-8ea7-91e1a62d6bf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f43af21-b0c5-466c-91bb-0f398f9c0f06",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice creating and reusing arrow functions while naming them descriptively and commenting their purpose.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 6 through 8..\n- Level 6:Students extract an inline event handler and rewrite it as a named arrow function.\n- Level 7: With AI support, students find repeated code and refactor it into a single, reusable function.\n- Level 8: Students choose a context and create as many well-named, functional arrow functions as possible within the time limit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, prompt them to describe what their function does and why they chose that name. Ask: “What does your function’s name tell you about its purpose?” or “How could you reuse this function in a different context?”\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Creating Functions",
        "progression_name": "Creating Functions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For Level 8, ensure students have at least 10 minutes to explore. Encourage them to use AI to brainstorm new function ideas or to test whether their function names clearly describe what the code does before implementing it.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 6–8 to formatively assess student understanding of the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Write and call arrow functions correctly.\n- Refactor repeated code into reusable functions.\n- Use clear, descriptive names and appropriate comments.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f43af21-b0c5-466c-91bb-0f398f9c0f06",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81a54c4f-6683-4d82-9e8c-d833fc288d60",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81a54c4f-6683-4d82-9e8c-d833fc288d60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "906d3768-b038-4e49-883d-a22a88c2fe9d",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Developers often review each other’s code to make sure it’s readable and understandable. Your job is to leave short, kind, and specific comments that help your peers see how clearly their code communicates its purpose.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "906d3768-b038-4e49-883d-a22a88c2fe9d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7786776f-3d05-408c-b7aa-ed198fdc5e06",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** At the end of the Mini-Hackathon, have students rotate to view 2–3 peers’ projects. Provide physical sticky notes for feedback. Their comments should focus on clarity, naming, and alignment between the function’s name, comment, and behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Remind students to use descriptive language in their feedback. If they’re unsure what to say, prompt them with: “Is the name of the function clear?”, “Can you tell what this code does without reading every line?”, “Does the comment match the code’s behavior?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, students could update one of their functions based on peer feedback, demonstrating responsiveness to review — a nice parallel to the QA process.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7786776f-3d05-408c-b7aa-ed198fdc5e06",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07051e72-b30d-44fc-ba74-53a99e26a8ca",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07051e72-b30d-44fc-ba74-53a99e26a8ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5952962b-eb0f-4e4b-a6a5-cd4d6b8376da",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does writing clear, well-named functions make it easier for other people—or AI—to test, debug, and reuse your code?*\n- *What did you notice when reviewing a peer’s code that made it easier (or harder) to understand?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Highlight that clear, descriptive function names support readability, reuse, and testing. Students should recognize that part of being a QA engineer or collaborator is ensuring that code is easy to understand for both humans and AI tools that analyze it.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, have students identify one specific feedback comment they gave or received that improved clarity. Reinforce that peer and AI feedback both play a role in ensuring code is maintainable and reliable.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5952962b-eb0f-4e4b-a6a5-cd4d6b8376da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee592697-7c51-4994-b713-5c0c738433af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b4df57d-6a0d-4e94-8af9-1fcc26bce606",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students discuss their responses to the Question of the Day with a partner. After a few minutes, ask for volunteers to share with the class. Encourage students to use examples from their code today — such as how they renamed a function, created a reusable arrow function, or refactored repeated code — to explain how they made their code cleaner and easier to reuse.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give general answers (like “I used functions”), prompt them to be more specific by asking follow-up questions such as:\n- “What change made your code easier for someone else to understand?”\n- “How did naming or reusing a function help simplify your program?”\n- “How might AI tools benefit from code that’s clearly named and organized?”\n\nThis reinforces both human and AI readability while helping students articulate what clean and reusable really mean in practice.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b4df57d-6a0d-4e94-8af9-1fcc26bce606",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b7d7dd47-636c-401b-b3cf-f9b3c9abae6c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78b270d6-0571-4854-8385-66ff7eb46925",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78b270d6-0571-4854-8385-66ff7eb46925",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b7d7dd47-636c-401b-b3cf-f9b3c9abae6c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3a8ed4c-964f-42a5-8e10-f6298c38ec38",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3a8ed4c-964f-42a5-8e10-f6298c38ec38",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b7d7dd47-636c-401b-b3cf-f9b3c9abae6c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4abc1bb-930f-4a87-b432-1c20e93da2ab",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4abc1bb-930f-4a87-b432-1c20e93da2ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b7d7dd47-636c-401b-b3cf-f9b3c9abae6c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f52b4712-3c98-4238-b4db-8bf9677c6433",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What functionality of YouTube does this flowchart represent? What users would want the condition (yellow diamond) to evaluate to “Yes”? “No”?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that apps make decisions based on user input or app data, similar to the flowchart. Examples include YouTube deciding which videos to suggest next or login screens checking if username and password match. This helps students recognize that apps are full of decision-making points and anchors abstract conditionals in contexts they know. It also helps them understand that user input can determine the user experience, for example turning on Autoplay might help some users and frustrate others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f52b4712-3c98-4238-b4db-8bf9677c6433",
        "lesson_activity.key": "27a0d74d-a484-4a79-8bab-020d22f79ca3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e693e4a4-fbda-4c33-857a-ca622ff1fad7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students practice modeling decisions as flowcharts and understanding how those decisions can be represented in their code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 6.\n- Level 1: Students use Sketch Lab to build their own flowchart.\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for flowcharts.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of flowcharts to the vocab free response.\n- Level 4: Students review the panel level to understand how conditionals look in JavaScript.\n- Level 5: Students read the vocabulary definition for conditional statements and booleans.\n- Level 6: Students add their own explanation or example of conditional statements to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are including all key components including a start, process, decision, and outcome. Each flowchart must incorporate at least one variable and at least one function. Help students who are not sure of what to flowchart by asking prompting questions like: \"What decisions did you make to get to class today?\" or \"In what order did those steps occur?\"\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Crafting Conditionals with Flowcharts",
        "progression_name": "Creating Conditionals with Flowcharts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e693e4a4-fbda-4c33-857a-ca622ff1fad7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1608ddea-2dea-4f51-a760-83bf2f2e3dac",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Flowchart"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1608ddea-2dea-4f51-a760-83bf2f2e3dac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "890c48b7-e550-4f56-a477-2ff34291bd68",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Conditional Statements"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "890c48b7-e550-4f56-a477-2ff34291bd68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44f997bf-f961-44ba-bff9-79d1a501d005",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Conditional Statement",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are feeling confused by the flowchart elements, have them start with the yellow diamond decision point. Turn their decision into a question with (at least) two outcomes. Then have them work backwards to figure out any functions and variables needed for making that decision.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity as a formative check of how well students understand flowcharting conditional statements. Look for evidence that students are:\n- Able to use diamond shapes to represent a decision point\n- Flowcharting least two outcomes as a result of a decision point (e.g. Yes/No)\n- Able to incorporate at least one variable and at least one function.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44f997bf-f961-44ba-bff9-79d1a501d005",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4c986cb2-03e3-4e9f-9e37-c4ca2aff7f44",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4c986cb2-03e3-4e9f-9e37-c4ca2aff7f44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3af94ebf-9161-4bd9-8c92-e29967f1d260",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students partner up to trace through the different paths of their partner’s flowchart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Does your partner’s flowchart make sense? Are the decision points clear?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to articulate how their partner’s flowchart represents a decision-making process and receive constructive feedback on the clarity and completeness of their own flowchart. This peer feedback reinforces the concepts of algorithmic thinking and visual logic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define flowchart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define conditional statement.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define Boolean.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3af94ebf-9161-4bd9-8c92-e29967f1d260",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "183f3e96-a7ee-4b2c-a3bb-dcfb604600ea",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Translate flowchart decisions into JavaScript if/else statements and use AI as a coding partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students review the flowcharts they made in Level 1. They should draft JavaScript if/else statements on Level 7 to represent the conditionals in their flowchart, making sure to incorporate at least one variable and at least one function into their code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Guide students to compare the flowchart logic directly to the code they are writing. Encourage them to use the AI to debug by asking how to debug incorrect conditionals or errors (e.g., 'Why is this conditional set up incorrectly? Help me fix it.')\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Turn Flowcharts to Code",
        "progression_name": "Turning a Flowchart into Code",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that the AI is a tool for learning and debugging, not a replacement for understanding. Encourage students to explain their code and the AI's suggestions to each other to make sure they understand it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "183f3e96-a7ee-4b2c-a3bb-dcfb604600ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "219ee27b-ac96-4f50-aa3b-2ebd3815b43f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "219ee27b-ac96-4f50-aa3b-2ebd3815b43f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a2d7397-0fb4-47b8-8beb-b9e7e9aa16b8",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Consider an if-else conditional you included in your code. Who benefits from the decision evaluating to “True”? Who benefits from it evaluating to “False”?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on who benefits from rules that are implemented. This can be the user themself having a better or worse outcome, or someone else affected by the decision point. For example, consider a decision point based on the business hours of a restaurant. If a customer arrives after a restaurant’s kitchen has closed and is not able to order food, it creates a worse outcome for the customer but a better outcome for the restaurant staff who can close on time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete the Level 8.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Students are interpreting a conditional statement and answering a question about it. As students read over the question, ask them to explain their reasoning.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses to gauge whether they can clearly explain how to debug conditional statements.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a2d7397-0fb4-47b8-8beb-b9e7e9aa16b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "61754384-fa0d-46fc-ad2f-d53e49373c0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3ea7069-5a7e-4c28-b1c9-d8d15b363415",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 9 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3ea7069-5a7e-4c28-b1c9-d8d15b363415",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a476d642-91c6-4063-81b5-0dc918a69891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b38dfd3-6448-43aa-b08b-ca9bb76b9652",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b38dfd3-6448-43aa-b08b-ca9bb76b9652",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a476d642-91c6-4063-81b5-0dc918a69891"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77ae46b3-aff7-4eea-bf72-5bc401ead72f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to imagine they are shopping online for clothing and click the \"Add to Cart\" button. Prompt them to think about what information the website needs to process this request. Record their ideas on the board. Ask students to consider what would happen if one of the pieces of information (like size, color, or quantity) was missing when they clicked \"Add to Cart\". Guide them to understand that functions need specific information to work correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77ae46b3-aff7-4eea-bf72-5bc401ead72f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6284fa36-fc19-4a92-9758-8c76bda53ef5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42f5c589-f26f-4805-8474-96e61548a110",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students will extend their flowchart knowledge by introducing new shapes for inputs (arguments) passed into a function and visualize how parameters enter and move through a function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What new symbol do you notice? What is its purpose?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Introduce the blue rounded box symbol in flowcharts as representing input (parameters) for a process (function). Students should be able tp identify the inputs (color, size, material, and quantity) the process (function) requires. The purpose of these parameters is to make the Add to Cart process more reusable; there can be a single function that accounts for a wide variety of different shirts and properties.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define parameter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do parameters make a single function able to handle different variations (like different shirt sizes or colors)?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should articulate that parameters allow a single function to be reused with different data, making the code more efficient and adaptable. Guide the discussion to highlight that instead of writing separate functions for 'add small shirt' and 'add large shirt,' one function can handle both by accepting a 'size' parameter.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42f5c589-f26f-4805-8474-96e61548a110",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26291e63-8d8b-4f8e-a864-987548aae977"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7b6a739-8e90-4154-a8f8-66ba83808aa4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Students will complete Lesson 6, Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Analyze a flowchart in Sketch Lab to identified repeated code that might be refactored into one parameterized function.\n- Level 2: Write clear, beginner-friendly docs for your refactored function from Sketch Lab.\n- Level 3: Trace the user flow within an actual application.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Writing Functions with Parameters",
        "progression_name": "Exploring Functions with Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7b6a739-8e90-4154-a8f8-66ba83808aa4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26291e63-8d8b-4f8e-a864-987548aae977"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4353b21b-be7e-49ea-9c98-d5b537a6a58a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 4. Explain that students will now create their own parameterized function. Provide examples of contexts they could use (e.g., rendering a card, setting a status, updating a score). Instruct them to document their function in Markdown and then use AI Suggest Mode to help draft the JavaScript code, emphasizing that AI will provide hints rather than full solutions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Provide support and guidance as students design their functions. Offer suggestions for different contexts and help them refine their documentation and code. Encourage them to experiment with different parameters and their effects.",
        "progression_name": "Writing Functions with Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4353b21b-be7e-49ea-9c98-d5b537a6a58a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26291e63-8d8b-4f8e-a864-987548aae977"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adf6d5b4-11f9-44dc-bc19-199ebb5a1112",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adf6d5b4-11f9-44dc-bc19-199ebb5a1112",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26291e63-8d8b-4f8e-a864-987548aae977"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f76f8d7-ac59-442c-a8c6-a29c9133d882",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Parameter",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students who struggle with starting, provide sentence frames for the Markdown documentation, such as \"This function ______ when given ______.\" For students who prefer visual planning, allow them to create a flowchart first in Sketch Lab before documenting.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f76f8d7-ac59-442c-a8c6-a29c9133d882",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26291e63-8d8b-4f8e-a864-987548aae977"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "977c2f4f-45ef-4d7d-b299-2c98d0c22c65",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "977c2f4f-45ef-4d7d-b299-2c98d0c22c65",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26291e63-8d8b-4f8e-a864-987548aae977"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed7da37a-bbde-4262-b779-8ca4594e468d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Facilitate a brief discussion about working with parameters. Prompt students to share specific examples of how their functions that they created were made more reusable and flexible by adding one or more parameters. Then ask students to share their experiences using the AI tool for coding assistance. Encourage them to discuss both the benefits (e.g., faster code generation, debugging hints) and the drawbacks (e.g., potential for incorrect suggestions, difficulty understanding the generated code).\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Normalize the idea that AI is a tool and not always perfect. Emphasize the importance of critical thinking and verifying AI-generated output. Discuss how AI can be a powerful learning aid when used effectively.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed7da37a-bbde-4262-b779-8ca4594e468d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "26291e63-8d8b-4f8e-a864-987548aae977"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8790d126-4708-454e-90ac-b1d71fd46263",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8790d126-4708-454e-90ac-b1d71fd46263",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ed379d5-4cb9-4d05-b76f-ade6aae4a662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5e63518-2b79-4143-a4c5-43a22e9e12d0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5e63518-2b79-4143-a4c5-43a22e9e12d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ed379d5-4cb9-4d05-b76f-ade6aae4a662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2593501a-6b56-4b74-a7ae-e290d31e35cb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the Understanding Assistive Technology video of a user with visual needs navigating a web page.\n\nQuestion to consider with the video:\n- What made navigating the site easy or hard for this user?\n- What changes could developers make in the code to improve this experience?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect the idea that accessibility isn’t about adding effects—it’s about designing code that communicates dynamically with different users. This prepares them to use variables, parameters, and event listeners to build pages that respond to user needs. Encourage students to refer to what they noticed in the video—how the screen reader described or skipped elements, and how the user interacted through swipes, double-taps, or the rotor feature.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2593501a-6b56-4b74-a7ae-e290d31e35cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3269d70c-13b5-484b-a4b2-8265d0691ee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71a5ff84-fa74-48b8-bdb4-67023baa8303",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Accessibility means making sure your site works for everyone—not just how it looks, but how it responds.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71a5ff84-fa74-48b8-bdb4-67023baa8303",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3269d70c-13b5-484b-a4b2-8265d0691ee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1ceef39-2d94-4238-b75f-bf1b1dca987c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1ceef39-2d94-4238-b75f-bf1b1dca987c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3269d70c-13b5-484b-a4b2-8265d0691ee0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4f29189-3d5c-4be9-8aaa-8cfbe1ec85d0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize common accessibility challenges that affect users and understand why improving accessibility is part of enhancing overall user experience. This understanding sets up the role of UX Designers, who focus on solving these issues through human-centered design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce common accessibility issues.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Accessibility",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite students to identify which of these issues they’ve seen or experienced themselves.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4f29189-3d5c-4be9-8aaa-8cfbe1ec85d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23692d9b-7e3a-4bf3-8cce-53e648792076",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23692d9b-7e3a-4bf3-8cce-53e648792076",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d76168b-561b-4e59-9d64-132b75efb5a8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the video, we saw one user experience accessibility challenges when navigating a page. These are some of the most common accessibility barriers people encounter online — like low contrast, missing image descriptions, small text, or unclear layouts. Addressing these kinds of issues isn’t just about fixing problems — it’s about improving the experience for everyone.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d76168b-561b-4e59-9d64-132b75efb5a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7abb70c-a5c5-46c8-ab24-790078f4f528",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how UX Designers make sure websites and apps are easy to use and accessible for everyone. They research how people interact with technology, spot problems that make it harder to use, and design ways to fix them. Sometimes they use AI tools to test for accessibility issues—like checking contrast or detecting missing image descriptions—but they still rely on human judgment to make the best decisions about layout, tone, and user needs.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7abb70c-a5c5-46c8-ab24-790078f4f528",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa89dc3b-91c2-4183-b2de-d613582c4ad3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll think a little bit like a UX Designer as you plan and code small improvements that make web pages more responsive and inclusive. We'll connect accessibility concepts to the JavaScript and DOM manipulation techniques we'll be practicing.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa89dc3b-91c2-4183-b2de-d613582c4ad3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67a5ddd4-c953-488a-900a-a470d95f6373",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Building More Dynamic Pages activity guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67a5ddd4-c953-488a-900a-a470d95f6373",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "126701a2-0f29-49aa-97d8-dd914f5d88b2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’re going to be doing the work of a UX designer during an accessibility pass. You’ll spot issues, connect them to user benefits, and identify which exact element or style in the DOM a developer could update to create a better experience for that user.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "126701a2-0f29-49aa-97d8-dd914f5d88b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83f91389-5353-4da5-b152-6616e81b5c98",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you do this, focus not only on what needs fixing but also on how code can make the change happen dynamically—through variables, event listeners, or other interactive elements that respond to user needs.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83f91389-5353-4da5-b152-6616e81b5c98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1a40cf8b-40ae-4190-bca3-9e9b63d9e1ed",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Send students to Level 1 to complete the a T-chart in their activity guide. The three columns should include:\n- Concept: Accessibility principle (e.g., High Contrast)\n- User Benefit: What improvement this creates for the user (e.g., Readability for users with visual impairments or bright lighting)\n- DOM Hook: The part of the code or element that can be updated to achieve that change (e.g., Toggle a class like theme-dark or high-contrast)\n\nRemind students that the T-Chart connects human needs to the specific technical hooks developers use to meet those needs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask: “Which code element or variable might control that behavior?” or “How could this improvement be made responsive instead of static?” Encourage them to think about how code logic helps meet the user’s needs, not just what problems exist.\n",
        "progression_name": "Accessibility Concepts",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to connect accessibility concepts to the DOM, prompt them to think about changeable attributes. For example:\n- Color contrast → class or style updates\n- Font size → variable or CSS property controlled by input\n- Layout clarity → toggling visibility or simplifying content through event listeners\n\nEncourage students to share one “dynamic helper” idea they think could make a big difference for users.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1a40cf8b-40ae-4190-bca3-9e9b63d9e1ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b228498-05cb-4d2b-8288-0a319b250059",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b228498-05cb-4d2b-8288-0a319b250059",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ac542d9-9386-4254-9004-e0706b4fc941",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even though AI tools can identify accessibility issues, it’s still up to UX designers and developers to decide which changes actually improve the user experience. Accessibility is more than fixing problems—it’s about designing features that respond to people’s needs. In today’s activity, you’ll redesign a webpage to make it more dynamic and inclusive for a specific user.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Accessibility Redesign",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ac542d9-9386-4254-9004-e0706b4fc941",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47d1438e-aedc-4326-ba2e-68ef275087fa",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Levels 2 and 3.\n- Level 2: Students choose a starter website and user persona, then plan which accessibility improvements to make in their activity guide.\n- Level 3: Students review the accessibility feature menu to learn about possible changes and decide which ones best serve their user.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask: “What will your feature respond to—a click, an input, or another event?” and “How could this feature adapt dynamically as a user interacts with the page?” Remind students that dynamic features use variables and event handlers to respond to user actions, rather than static, one-time edits.\n",
        "progression_name": "Redesign for a User"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47d1438e-aedc-4326-ba2e-68ef275087fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1471a126-cd68-4f71-9aea-0939058114c5",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Menu of Accessibility Features",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to design for response and interaction. Prompt students to connect each improvement to a real user benefit, not just a visual change.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity to formatively assess how well students demonstrate the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Use JavaScript to change styles, swap images, or show/hide elements in their project.\n- Apply variables and parameters to make those DOM changes dynamic and event-driven.\n- Revise interactions to improve accessibility and user experience, and explain how they balanced trade-offs between usability, design, and complexity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1471a126-cd68-4f71-9aea-0939058114c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0d18377-7b4e-4733-8953-5ce86e4c7d28",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0d18377-7b4e-4733-8953-5ce86e4c7d28",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71052c2e-bd6c-4f30-8b56-3b45d60fc2c8",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students rotate through the classroom (or share projects digitally) to demo their accessibility features. As they explore, they should identify:\n- The change: What was added or improved?\n- The behavior: Does it respond dynamically to user actions or settings (e.g., slider, toggle, user input)?\n- The impact: Who benefits and how does it improve accessibility?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students rotate, prompt with questions that push students to think beyond visuals and into the logic of interaction and inclusivity, such as: “How does this feature change based on what the user does?”, “Would this improvement help more than one kind of user?” and “What trade-offs might this feature create?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, wrap up with a quick share-out:\n- Which feature impressed you most and why?\n- Did you notice any trade-offs between usability and complexity?\n\nUse this as a bridge into the final discussion or reflection — it reinforces accessibility as both a design and coding challenge.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71052c2e-bd6c-4f30-8b56-3b45d60fc2c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e1a3464-9745-4d67-8bcc-721762a14a7f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9189ee7a-6a3a-435f-80de-56b577177177",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What trade-offs did you have to make when improving your page’s accessibility or interactivity?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that real-world design often involves trade-offs between accessibility, aesthetics, and functionality. They reflect on how they balanced competing priorities to best serve their users—just like real UX designers do. Encourage students to share examples where they simplified, redesigned, or adjusted their features to improve accessibility (for example, reducing motion or increasing contrast). Use this discussion to reinforce the third lesson objective: Revising interactions to improve accessibility and balance trade-offs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 4 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9189ee7a-6a3a-435f-80de-56b577177177",
        "lesson_activity.key": "40a226ea-3a83-4816-b3eb-87e4b0e9555e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "701adead-5c27-4957-bde4-bbf58f21ba05",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "701adead-5c27-4957-bde4-bbf58f21ba05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "40a226ea-3a83-4816-b3eb-87e4b0e9555e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "610b8a69-ab8e-4818-8745-bf5e5a2e3a79",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to follow the paths on the flowchart that apply to them and complete the corresponding task. Emphasize that they should be ready to explain the path they took.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Based on what a classmate is wearing, which path would they follow in the flowchart? What ending task would they complete?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice tracing nested decisions step-by-step, recognizing how each condition leads to a specific outcome. This builds understanding that nested conditionals evaluate one condition inside another to reach a final result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "610b8a69-ab8e-4818-8745-bf5e5a2e3a79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2899524d-0c31-42e6-ba40-0afa0caefcc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "978852e2-d176-4d65-a234-8c28d59b020f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build foundational understanding of nested if/else statements by translating a simple flowchart into working code. They’ll learn how each decision branch connects to a UI change, define what nested conditionals are, and use AI support to debug and confirm that all paths behave as expected.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Nested Conditionals activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3, where they translate a provided flowchart into nest if/else statements with AI assistance.\n- Level 1: Students will label and map a provided flowchart into nested if/else statements.\n- Level 2: Students will read the definition nested conditional.\n- Level 3: Students will define nested conditional in their own words.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students implement their nested if/else logic, check that their code directly maps to the flowchart. Ask them to explain how each branch changes the UI and how they’re using test cases to verify correctness.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Flowchart to Code",
        "progression_name": "From Mini-Flowchart to Nested If/Else"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "978852e2-d176-4d65-a234-8c28d59b020f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eaf1674f-f975-421b-afc3-4eccec292b7f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Nested Conditional",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Highlight the roles of a Front-End Developer (implementing the code) and a QA Engineer (executing tests and logging bugs) in the software development process.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eaf1674f-f975-421b-afc3-4eccec292b7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5aad1917-4591-4668-ad89-b3ca4fb91daa",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5aad1917-4591-4668-ad89-b3ca4fb91daa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e79b1f4c-9c47-4097-9ccd-ced01d98fa11",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does nesting conditions add depth to our logic?*\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that nesting creates a hierarchy of decisions, allowing for a more granular control flow that mirrors complex real-world scenarios and flowcharts.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e79b1f4c-9c47-4097-9ccd-ced01d98fa11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7828999-e30e-4aa7-8d07-4fccc1159aef",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of nested if/else logic by planning, coding, and testing a multi-decision feature. They’ll connect their flowchart to working code, use AI to debug and refine logic, and verify that all four paths behave as intended through systematic testing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 4 and 5 by planning nested decisions and finally implement them in code. They will use scaffolds like the Test Cases table in their activity guide and AI support to refine their work.\n\n- Level 4: Students will plan a feature by creating a flowchart with nested decisions and test cases.\n- Level 5: Students will implement their plan in code, debug using test cases, and refine with AI support.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students implement their nested if/else logic, check that their code directly maps to the flowchart. Ask them to explain how each branch changes their web app and how they’re using test cases to verify correctness.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Plan, Code, and Test",
        "progression_name": "Plan in Sketch Lab"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7828999-e30e-4aa7-8d07-4fccc1159aef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e93654f9-3ddf-439e-a1fd-d2d747436506",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Code and Test"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e93654f9-3ddf-439e-a1fd-d2d747436506",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50f3f1a1-a532-4142-b6ac-f363fc2798c3",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50f3f1a1-a532-4142-b6ac-f363fc2798c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2a58791-6d70-4258-928c-4ecdc90c0132",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What paths in your flowchart did AI miss?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students may notice that the AI assumed one condition mattered more (e.g., finished movie = true) — a small example of bias in logic or oversimplification.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt students to connect this debugging step to real-world examples of biased or incomplete algorithms (e.g., recommendation systems showing only one kind of result).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2a58791-6d70-4258-928c-4ecdc90c0132",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bd1c850-9636-4f6c-95b8-0bd46dd31c61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "067df326-aef8-426a-b7d0-c5580192c533",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the sentences as an exit ticket:\n- 'One thing a nested if/else enables in my app is ___.'\n- In my flowchart, branch ___ maps to this exact code line: ___.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have a pair project their flowchart and briefly trace how a specific input traverses branches to a UI result in their code. Bonus: 'A test case that broke then passed after debugging was ___ (why?).'\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "067df326-aef8-426a-b7d0-c5580192c533",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12c03669-d025-4ac2-a9b9-21c6cb9be903"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d1cb45b-9c68-40c7-a751-501d4d727777",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 5 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d1cb45b-9c68-40c7-a751-501d4d727777",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12c03669-d025-4ac2-a9b9-21c6cb9be903"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "986f3a17-811d-4f28-8409-533ac8aa44e1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the two example \"style rules\" on the slide. Have students come up with their own rule, using either 'and' or 'or' and discuss it with a partner. Emphasize that these should be school-appropriate, positive descriptors only.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "986f3a17-811d-4f28-8409-533ac8aa44e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2d4c260-9534-4753-ad72-ff05eba95b61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "892075ad-9f89-4615-8be7-77915050f886",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we're going to explore how combining conditional statements using AND and OR can make our programs more robust. Real web apps often need to check more than one condition at a time. Simple if-else statements can only handle one test, but combining conditions helps your code make smarter decisions — like showing confidential information only if a user is logged in AND verified.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "892075ad-9f89-4615-8be7-77915050f886",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2d4c260-9534-4753-ad72-ff05eba95b61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88176173-ef9f-4ade-ac1c-b780551faeb5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88176173-ef9f-4ade-ac1c-b780551faeb5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b2d4c260-9534-4753-ad72-ff05eba95b61"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a111e859-cf98-4eac-9df1-190ec2593da6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will be able to explore web pages that use combined conditional statements with && (AND) and || (OR).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Combining Conditions with AND/OR activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 8. Explain that each checkbox sets a Boolean value (True or False). Have students work in pairs to predict the message outcome for each combination of checkboxes. Then have them toggle the checkboxes to test their predictions and record their observations in the activity guide.\n- Level 1: Test the AND operator to produce a message for all four outcomes: TT, TF, FT, FF.\n- Level 2: Test the OR operator to produce a message for all four outcomes: TT, TF, FT, FF.\n- Level 3: Students read the vocabulary definition for AND (&&).\n- Level 4: Students add their own explanation or example of AND (&&) to the vocab free response.\n- Level 5: Students read the vocabulary definition for OR (||).\n- Level 6: Students add their own explanation or example of OR (||) to the vocab free response.\n- Level 7: Students read the vocabulary definition for truth table.\n- Level 8: Students add their own explanation or example of truth table to the vocab free response.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
        "progression_name": "Using AND and OR"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a111e859-cf98-4eac-9df1-190ec2593da6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31cc14bd-0d58-461e-a9af-408d4aff85b1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: AND (&&)"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31cc14bd-0d58-461e-a9af-408d4aff85b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "abbde58e-2a90-4d07-a59d-404ee9778a07",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: OR (||)"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "abbde58e-2a90-4d07-a59d-404ee9778a07",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2fde98b-9f9e-433b-beb9-6ebe1b94be0f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Truth Table",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider demonstrating for students how to toggle ON/OFF the different checkboxes, while explaining how each operator works. Make sure students see how && requires both conditions to be true; || requires at least one condition to be true. Call on a few students to come up with their own conditional statements to test out the logic.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2fde98b-9f9e-433b-beb9-6ebe1b94be0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7fa3c4af-fc0e-451e-8b30-0ed62fc7f8ee",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7fa3c4af-fc0e-451e-8b30-0ed62fc7f8ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "724c53ab-bb95-4e6e-9956-f76788afbcf8",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does changing the operator from && to || affect the outcome?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to articulate how AND and OR operators change the logic of conditional statements. Guide the discussion towards understanding that AND requires all conditions to be met, while OR requires only one.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define AND (&&).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define OR (||).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define truth table.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "724c53ab-bb95-4e6e-9956-f76788afbcf8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe1155e7-f9e5-43c3-be2d-2fcad8ff93cd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will create a flowchart to map out operator-based conditions. They will turn their flowchart into code in the following level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Levels 9 and 10. Instruct students to choose a scenario and flowchart their rules in Sketch Lab. Students must ensure that there are outcomes for all four test cases (TT, TF, FT, FF) for their chosen context. Then, have them turn their flowcharts into code. They should swap in the appropriate AND (&&) or OR (||) operators and adjust any comparison operators (e.g., >= 13).\n- Level 9: Flowchart your context in Sketch Lab. Include at least two decision points and outcomes for all 4 test cases.\n- Level 10: Convert the Sketch Lab flowchart into code. Test out the code for all 4 possible outcomes.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Coding a Multi-Condition Flowchart ",
        "progression_name": "Creating a Multi-Decision Flowchart"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe1155e7-f9e5-43c3-be2d-2fcad8ff93cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ae88c77-c7d3-4d6b-a460-38fe23bf6643",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Code Your Rule",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider having students attempt the longest chained conditional (multiple && and || operators) they can create. Encourage them to feed it to AI Tutor to create edge cases for them to test.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ae88c77-c7d3-4d6b-a460-38fe23bf6643",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7b0b34bb-50f2-440e-9091-32bb9cd4c3b9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50e8b49c-c0d3-4083-b2bc-0b70429dc200",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 11 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
